From ecf5d2d409224f7f2547af3cd6568a427e1e489a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JohnnyC96 <54557697+JohnnyC96@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 06:31:24 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] VU1: float fast path without long double, cheaper per-cycle
 bookkeeping

The FMAC flag/clamp check recomputed every lane in long double. On MSVC
long double is double; on arm64 Android it is IEEE quad done in software
(87 soft-float library calls in ps2_vu1_core.cpp, ~33x a double lane on
x86 soft-fp). It now runs in double, which is what every Windows result
and the 120 recorded VU1 captures already used:
- the product of two VU floats is exact in double; a + b*c rounds once
  (round toward zero), keeping sign and zero, never crossing FLT_MIN;
  the only quad/double difference was a sum within one double step above
  FLT_MAX (FLT_MAX + 2^70: quad reported overflow, Windows did not).
- ps2_vu1_detail.h turns floating-point contraction off for the VU
  files: NDK clang (-ffp-contract=on) fused acc + a*b into fmadd (67 in
  ps2_vu1_upper.cpp, 34 in ps2_vu1_lower.cpp), one rounding instead of
  two, so Android VU1 results differed from Windows in the last bits.

Per-cycle overheads, results unchanged:
- commitReadyPipelines: returns before the earliest queued ready cycle;
  per queue, visits only queues whose next ready cycle has come and only
  their valid entries (bit mask), in the same order.
- Decoded-pair cache: a new code generation bumps an epoch and pairs
  decode on first use, instead of re-decoding all 2,048 pairs; the pair
  is returned by reference.
- XGKICK: the 64 KiB packet buffer moved out of XgkickPipeline, so
  m_xgkick = {} (every XGKICK and execute) no longer clears 64 KiB; a
  qword that does not wrap is one memcpy instead of 16 modulo copies.
- normalizeOperand inline in the header; VI ready/write loops walk the
  set bits.

Tests (ps2x VU1 suite, 3 cases): FLT_MAX + 2^70 (ADD and MADD) is no
overflow, FLT_MAX + 2^104 is; MADD -1 + (1+2^-12)^2 = 0x3A000000 (fused:
0x3A000400); denormal operand reads as zero, a -2^-149 result flushes to
-0 with Z/S/U. Fail without (GCC build, 80-bit long double): the
overflow case reports x overflow (MAC c000); with -mfma the MADD case
gives 0x3A000400. vucompare: the same 100/120 captures replay exactly.
