+// The FMAC result again in double, before it is rounded to float, so the Z/S/U/O flags and the clamp see the
+// value the float result came from (float overflow saturates to infinity, underflow to a denormal or zero).
+// Double is exact for every single product of two VU floats (48-bit mantissa, exponent within +-256) and for
+// sums whose operands are close in magnitude; otherwise it rounds (round toward zero, run() sets it), which
+// preserves sign and zero and never crosses FLT_MIN. It was long double until patch 0045: on MSVC long double
+// IS double, so the Windows results (and the 120 recorded captures) were already these; on arm64 Android long
+// double is IEEE quad done in software (~87 library calls in this file) and differed from Windows only where a
+// sum lands within one double rounding step above FLT_MAX (FLT_MAX + 2^70: quad says overflow, double does not).
 bool VU1Interpreter::calculateFmacExactResult(uint32_t component,
-                                               long double &result) const
+                                               double &result) const
 {
     const uint32_t upper = m_currentUpperInstruction;
